Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Your body is sending a clear signal — it's time to rest and recover.",
  "summary": "This period tells a consistent story across every muscle group: strength is down, you feel run down, your joints ache, and your sessions are getting worse. These are classic signs of accumulated fatigue that has outpaced your recovery. At 28 years old with a healthy BMI of 23.5, your baseline capacity for recovery is strong — but no one is immune to overreaching. The encouraging news is that this is very fixable. A structured deload now will not cost you your gains; it will protect them and set you up to come back stronger.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "status": "regressing",
    "notes": "Every single muscle group has declined in strength index this period. Chest dropped from 105 to 99, back from 104 to 98, legs from 104 to 97, shoulders from 103 to 99, biceps from 101 to 100, and triceps from 102 to 100. This is a broad, uniform regression — not a single lift stalling in isolation. When all muscle groups regress together in the same period, and the user simultaneously reports feeling run down with worsening sessions, the data strongly points to systemic fatigue rather than a technique or programming issue with any specific lift. Progressive overload is currently impossible to achieve in this fatigued state."
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "biceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Biceps volume sits at only 4% of total training volume (6 sets), which is notably low relative to triceps at 8.4% (6 sets) and back at 28%. Given the muscle-building goal, direct biceps work is underrepresented and may lag behind pulling muscles once recovery is restored."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "legs",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Leg volume at 22.4% and 12 sets is reasonable, but legs showed the steepest strength index drop this period (from 104 to 97, a 7-point fall), suggesting they may have been trained especially hard relative to recovery capacity."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": true,
  "deloadReason": "Every strength index has declined this period, joint aches are present, subjective fatigue is high, and session quality is deteriorating. These are the hallmarks of overreaching. A deload — typically reducing total sets by around 40-50% and dropping working weights to roughly 60-70% of normal — will allow the nervous system and connective tissue to recover without losing muscle. Attempting to push through at current volume and intensity risks injury and deeper regression.",
  "actionItems": [
    "Take a proper deload this coming period: cut your total sets to roughly 28-30 and reduce working weights to about 60-70% of your usual loads. Keep the movements the same but treat every set as submaximal and comfortable.",
    "Prioritise sleep and nutrition during the deload. Muscle is built during recovery, not during the session itself. Aim for at least 7-8 hours of sleep per night and ensure you are eating close to your maintenance calories with adequate protein (around 1.6-2g per kg of bodyweight, so roughly 115-145g per day at your current weight).",
    "After the deload, gradually rebuild volume over the following two periods rather than jumping straight back to 56 sets. This slower ramp-up will help you arrive at peak volume in a recovered state and resume progressive overload.",
    "Once back to full training, consider slightly increasing direct biceps work — adding 2-3 sets per period would bring it more in line with your other arm and pulling muscles and better support your muscle-building goal."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "User experienced full-body strength regression across all muscle groups this period alongside reported joint aches and worsening sessions, indicating clear overreaching; a deload was recommended before resuming progressive overload for muscle building."
}
